21:52:21 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re making a very fair point, and I think this comes down to what we mean by “we know.” If by “know” you mean “have a single, proven, step-by-step blueprint that leaves zero room for doubt,” then you’re right—we don’t know, and the existence of several competing theories is proof of that. There’s no in-between if the standard is absolute certainty. But the reason I (and many archaeologists) hesitate to just say “we don’t know” without qualification is that the phrase, in everyday use, implies a much deeper ignorance than actually exists. It makes it sound like we have no idea—that we’re staring at the pyramids and shrugging, which then gives equal footing to fringe ideas like aliens or lost advanced technology. In reality, we are not at zero. There’s a massive amount of established knowledge that narrows the possibilities to a specific class of solutions. Think of it like this: ### The “known” vs. “unknown” pie chart - **We know:** the material (limestone, granite), the quarry locations, the tools available (copper chisels, dolerite pounders, wooden mallets, ropes, sledges, boats), the organization of labor (skilled teams, not slaves), the fact that they moved blocks by water to the site, the use of ramps (remnants of construction ramps have been found at multiple sites), the use of lever holes and rock-cut sockets for scaffolding posts, and the likely timeframe (roughly 20 years). We also have experimental archaeology where modern teams have successfully moved and lifted multi-ton blocks using only ancient-style techniques. - **We don’t know:** the exact configuration of the ramps and lifting methods for the highest courses and the most precise placements of the Great Pyramid specifically. So when you have five competing theories, they all sit within that same narrow box of established facts. They all use the same known toolbox; they just arrange the tools in slightly different ways. The debate isn’t “ramps vs. water shafts vs. aliens